{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1789214457,"nonce":"RVkwre2cpXOt+HjNONRMs8PCiIQ=","title":"Heap closure environments","message":"A closure's environment is a stack slot, so a closure that escapes its\ndefining frame dangles. Python closures escape as a matter of course:\na decorator returns one, a callback is stored, `nonlocal` mutates a\ncell shared between a function and the closures it made. Every one of\nthose is use-after-return today.\n\nThis is the one piece of genuine runtime work the Python frontend\nneeds; everything else is frontend desugaring or an in-language type.\nIt is also already the named next step for parallel dispatch, which\nmust block on anything non-blocking for the same reason, so it is owed\ntwice.\n\nThe environment becomes a heap object with the captured variables as\nfields. Under the ownership default it is released by drop_insert like\nany other allocation; under the GC strategy it is traced. Captured\nmutable variables (`nonlocal`) need a cell the environment points at\nrather than a copied value, so two closures over one variable see one\nanother's writes.","files":null}]}